{"kind":"request","text":"The comment at cmd/gs/main.go:136 states the same trust-boundary error codex just caught in my documentation, and it is where I got it from.\n\nIt reads: \"No default name. The operator seeded here signs the genesis and every grant that follows, so who it is has to be a choice someone made.\" The second clause is wrong about the genesis. app.Init passes sequencerKey to kernel.Create, and kernel.Create hands that key to store.SignedCommit, so the sequencer key signs the genesis commit. The operator key signs act requests through Workspace.BuildActRequest, beginning with the bootstrap roster statement. The comment's conclusion — that the operator has to be a deliberate choice — is sound and the flag behaviour it defends is correct; only the reason given is inaccurate.\n\nFiled as its own request rather than fixed alongside the documentation, because that branch is markdown-only and mixing a code change into it would obscure both. Discovered while repairing docs/getting-started.md under request c3ac234a, where I had copied this sentence verbatim, trusting the comment.","body":{"conditions":"The comment at cmd/gs/main.go initCommand no longer claims the operator signs the genesis; it names the sequencer key as the genesis signer and the operator key as the signer of the operator's own acts, keeping the existing and correct rationale for why --operator has no default; the surrounding flag behaviour is unchanged; `make test` and `go vet` pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}